# NuttX audio: what a blocking `read()` actually waits on

**Headline correction up front:** in upstream NuttX (12.1.0, master, and open-vela's fork) the audio *upper half* does **not** implement a blocking read at all. `audio_read()` is a one-line passthrough to `lower->ops->read()`. There is no framework semaphore, no `audio_waitfordeq`, and the `DEQUEUE`/`COMPLETE` callbacks only post a message to a registered app message queue (and, in newer code, `poll_notify`). So the hang is inside **your** lower-half `read()`, not in the prebuilt upper half.

Also: the upper half **is** present as source in your tree — it lives at top-level **`nuttx/audio/audio.c`**, not `nuttx/drivers/audio/audio.c`. Upstream only keeps codec/DAC drivers in `drivers/audio/`. You can read the exact file you're linking against.

---

## Q1 — What does blocking `audio_read()` wait on?

Nothing in the framework. It forwards straight to the lower half.

`audio/audio.c:245` (nuttx-12.1.0):

```c
static ssize_t audio_read(FAR struct file *filep, FAR char *buffer, size_t buflen)
{
  FAR struct inode *inode = filep->f_inode;
  FAR struct audio_upperhalf_s *upper = inode->i_private;
  FAR struct audio_lowerhalf_s *lower = upper->dev;

  /* Audio read operations get passed directly to the lower-level */

  if (lower->ops->read != NULL)
    {
      return lower->ops->read(lower, buffer, buflen);
    }
  return 0;
}
```

Identical on master and in open-vela (`open-vela/nuttx` `audio/audio.c`, `audio_read` at line 293). The header even says read is non-standard: `include/nuttx/audio/audio.h:641` — *"Lower-half logic may support platform-specific read commands."*

